There's no universal winner — the right pick depends on your team, hiring needs, and how much structure you want out of the box.

:::intelligent-ui{v="1"}
root = Stack([cmp1, tbl1])
cmp1 = Comparison("React vs Vue", [reactItem, vueItem], true)
reactItem = {name:"React", attributes:["JSX, plain JavaScript logic","Unopinionated — you pick routing, state, data","Largest ecosystem and job market","Backed by Meta","React Native for real mobile apps"]}
vueItem = {name:"Vue", attributes:["Templates + single-file components","Progressive and batteries-included","Best-in-class docs, gentlest learning curve","Community-driven (Evan You)","No first-class native mobile story"]}
tbl1 = Table("Which to pick", ["Your situation", "Better fit"], [["Largest hiring pool and ecosystem", "React"], ["Fastest onboarding for new devs", "Vue"], ["Small-to-mid app shipped quickly", "Vue"], ["Large app with many contributors", "React"], ["Shared code with a mobile app", "React"], ["Team already knows one of them", "Stick with that one"]])
:::

The short version: Vue gets you productive faster and is genuinely pleasant to write, so it wins for solo devs and small teams moving quickly. React wins on leverage — more libraries, more hires, more Stack Overflow answers, and a mobile path via React Native. If you're building a long-lived product and expect to grow the team, React's friction is worth paying early; if you're optimizing for speed to a working app, Vue is the better deal. Neither choice is hard to reverse at the prototype stage, so don't let this stall you.
